/// The USB host controller driver `usb_host` and its class drivers (issue 164): `usb_host` owns the xHCI controller,
/// enumerates devices (through hubs) and hands each interface to one class driver; `usb_hid`, `usb_storage` and
/// `video_gw` (a camera's, 158) are its clients. A client's badge names the device class it may claim
/// (`mind::usb::BADGE_HID`, `BADGE_STORAGE`, `BADGE_VIDEO`), so a class driver reaches only its own interfaces (MC-3.3).
/// Data travel through the client's buffer, lent with `attach`.
package mind:usb@1.2.0;

interface usb {
    /// Lends the client's transfer buffer (at least `mind::usb::BUFFER` bytes) to this host driver instance. A restarted
    /// instance has none: every other call then fails with not-found and the client attaches again.
    attach: func(buffer: borrow<memory>) -> result<_, error-code>;
    /// Claims the next interface of the client's class that no client holds; returns its handle and writes its
    /// description (`mind::usb::Interface`) at the buffer's start. Errors: not-found, none now (a device may come later);
    /// invalid, no buffer is attached to this instance (it was restarted): attach, then claim again.
    claim: func() -> result<u32, error-code>;
    /// Gives a claimed interface back.
    release: func(handle: u32) -> result<_, error-code>;
    /// A control transfer on the device's endpoint 0; the data stage (up to 4096 bytes) is at the buffer's start.
    /// Returns the bytes transferred. Errors: not-found, the device is gone or the handle is not the client's; rights,
    /// a request for the device as a whole other than reading a descriptor (its address or configuration), or for
    /// another interface; invalid, the device refused (a stall); timeout, no answer.
    control: func(handle: u32, request-type: u8, request: u8, value: u16, index: u16, length: u16) -> result<u16, error-code>;
    /// A bulk transfer on the endpoint with address `address` (bit 7 set for IN) of the claimed interface, `length` bytes
    /// (up to 64 KiB) at `offset` in the buffer. Returns the bytes transferred. Errors as for `control`; after a
    /// stall the endpoint is reset on both sides, so the next transfer can run.
    bulk: func(handle: u32, address: u8, offset: u32, length: u32) -> result<u32, error-code>;
    /// The reports received on the interrupt IN endpoint with address `address` since the last call, written at the buffer's start as
    /// a length byte and the report each; returns their count. The first call starts polling the endpoint.
    reports: func(handle: u32, address: u8) -> result<u16, error-code>;
    /// 1.1 (211-DRV-0018): as `reports`, for an endpoint whose reports span several packets and end with a short one
    /// (a MacBook trackpad's finger data), up to `longest` bytes each (at most 512; 64 on xHCI for now). Each is written
    /// as two length bytes, low first, and the report. The first call starts polling with that length; a later one with
    /// another length is refused (invalid).
    reports-up-to: func(handle: u32, address: u8, longest: u16) -> result<u16, error-code>;
    /// 1.2 (158): selects alternate setting `alternate` of the claimed interface (SET_INTERFACE) and writes its
    /// description (`mind::usb::Interface`: that setting's endpoints, an isochronous one's packet as the descriptor's
    /// wMaxPacketSize) at the buffer's start. The setting's isochronous IN endpoint starts streaming into a queue of
    /// usb_host's (on EHCI; xHCI refuses such a setting for now); setting 0 stops it. Errors as for `control`; invalid,
    /// no such setting or one that cannot stream.
    select: func(handle: u32, alternate: u8) -> result<_, error-code>;
    /// 1.2 (158): the packets the selected setting's isochronous IN endpoint `address` received since the last call,
    /// written at `offset` in the buffer, at most `length` bytes: each as two length bytes, low first (bit 15 set when
    /// the controller reported an error for it), and the packet; empty packets are left out. Returns the bytes written.
    /// Packets that do not fit wait for the next call; while usb_host's queue is full, new ones are dropped (and counted).
    isochronous: func(handle: u32, address: u8, offset: u32, length: u32) -> result<u32, error-code>;
}
